>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/problems/">Fault Diagnosis</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Safety Warning</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Sperax Walking Pad Recall? The CPSC Warning, Explained</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        There is no Sperax recall. On 16 April 2026 the U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission (CPSC) issued a product safety warning telling owners of four Sperax walking pad and treadmill models to stop using them immediately and dispose of them, after the importer refused to agree to an acceptable recall. Here is exactly what the CPSC said, how to check your model, and what you can do about a refund.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 25, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on manufacturer manuals and service guidance, and the faults owners report most.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#what-to-do" class="btn-ghost">What to Do Now</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          People search for a &ldquo;Sperax recall&rdquo;, but what actually happened is different, and the difference
          matters for your money. A recall is announced with the company and comes with a remedy. What the CPSC issued for
          Sperax is a warning the agency published on its own because, in its words, the importer &ldquo;has refused to agree
          to an acceptable recall&rdquo;. Every fact on this page comes from that CPSC warning (Product Safety Warning
          26-415, dated 16 April 2026, published on cpsc.gov and in the CPSC&rsquo;s product safety warning listing) and from
          SaferProducts.gov, the CPSC&rsquo;s public reporting site. Where the CPSC did not say something, such as sale dates,
          prices or unit numbers, we say so rather than fill the gap. We checked the CPSC listing again on 25 November 2026;
          it still lists this as a warning, not a recall.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="Jump to">
          <h2>Jump to</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-cpsc-said">What the CPSC Actually Said</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warning-vs-recall">Warning vs Recall: Why the Word Matters for Your Refund</a></li>
            <li><a href="#models">Which Sperax Models, and How to Find Your Model Number</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-do">What Owners Should Do, Step by Step</a></li>
            <li><a href="#refund">Can You Get a Refund?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#dispose">How to Dispose of a Sperax Walking Pad</a></li>
            <li><a href="#report">How to Report a Problem to the CPSC</a></li>
            <li><a href="#used">Buying or Selling a Used Sperax</a></li>
            <li><a href="#hazards">Signs of the Same Hazards on Any Walking Pad</a></li>
            <li><a href="#safe-use">Walking Pad Safety Habits That Help on Any Brand</a></li>
            <li><a href="#alternatives">Choosing a Safer Replacement</a></li>
            <li><a href="#stay-updated">How to Check for Updates</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Sperax walking pads have not been recalled. On 16 April 2026 the CPSC warned consumers to stop using the
            Sperax Pro, Q1, RM-01 and RM-02 immediately and dispose of them, because they pose fall, burn and fire hazards and
            the importer refused to agree to an acceptable recall.</strong> Because there is no recall, there is no official
            refund, repair or replacement.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Stop using it now</strong> and unplug it. The CPSC&rsquo;s advice is to stop immediately, not to wait
            for a fix.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the model number</strong> on the label near the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a>: Pro, Q1, RM-01 or RM-02.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not sell it or give it away.</strong> The CPSC says so in plain words.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask the seller or platform you bought it from</strong> about a return or refund, before you dispose of it.</li>
            <li><strong>Report any incident</strong> at SaferProducts.gov.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Question</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What the CPSC says (warning 26-415, 16 April 2026)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Is it a recall?</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No. It is a product safety warning; the importer refused to agree to an acceptable recall</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Models</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Pro, Q1, RM-01 and RM-02</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hazards</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fall, burn and fire</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed and stopping reports</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">201 reports; at least 66 falls or injuries, including at least one concussion that needed medical attention</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heat and fire reports</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">573 reports of overheating, fires and other thermal incidents, including four reports of minor burns</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Importer</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Quanzhou Wentelai Import and Export Trading Co., Ltd., d/b/a Sperax</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sold at</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Online, including Amazon.com, Walmart.com, NewEgg.com and eBay.com</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">What to do</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using immediately and dispose of them; do not sell or give away</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remedy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None announced</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            If you are now shopping for a replacement, our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> guide covers current
            picks from other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-cpsc-said" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the CPSC Actually Said</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC&rsquo;s warning is short, and it is worth knowing exactly what it contains, because a lot of what you
            may read elsewhere goes further than the agency did. These are the points the warning makes, in the CPSC&rsquo;s
            own terms:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The instruction.</strong> The CPSC is &ldquo;warning consumers to stop using Sperax Walking Pads and
            Treadmills immediately because they pose fall, burn and fire hazards to consumers.&rdquo;</li>
            <li><strong>The falls.</strong> The products &ldquo;can suddenly and unexpectedly change speeds and come to an abrupt
            stop, causing consumers to lose their balance, fall, and suffer serious injuries.&rdquo; The CPSC is aware of 201
            reports of uncontrollable speed, abrupt stops and similar stability-related failures, resulting in at least 66
            falls or injuries, including at least one concussion that required medical attention.</li>
            <li><strong>The heat.</strong> The CPSC is also aware of 573 reports of overheating, fires and other thermal
            incidents, including four reports of minor burns.</li>
            <li><strong>The company.</strong> The importer, Quanzhou Wentelai Import and Export Trading Co., Ltd., doing
            business as Sperax, &ldquo;has refused to agree to an acceptable recall.&rdquo;</li>
            <li><strong>The company&rsquo;s response.</strong> The law requires the CPSC to include the manufacturer&rsquo;s
            comments, or a summary, with a release like this. The summary given is one sentence: &ldquo;The company objects to
            this press release.&rdquo;</li>
            <li><strong>Where they were sold and made.</strong> Online via e-commerce platforms, including Amazon.com,
            Walmart.com, NewEgg.com and eBay.com. The products were manufactured in China.</li>
            <li><strong>Disposal.</strong> The CPSC &ldquo;urges consumers to stop using the walking pads and treadmills
            immediately and dispose of them. Do not sell or give away these hazardous products.&rdquo;</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What the warning does <em>not</em> include matters too. It gives no number of units sold, no sale dates and no
            prices, and it does not say whether any other Sperax models are affected. It names four models and only four. If
            your Sperax is a different model, the warning does not cover it, but nor does it say that model is safe; it is
            simply silent. We have not added anything the CPSC did not say.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-vs-recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warning vs Recall: Why the Word Matters for Your Refund</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC publishes two different kinds of notice, and they are listed side by side on cpsc.gov under
            &ldquo;Recalls &amp; Product Safety Warnings&rdquo;. They lead to very different outcomes for an owner.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4"></th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Recall</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Product safety warning (Sperax)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Who announces it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The CPSC together with the company</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The CPSC alone; the company did not agree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remedy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually a refund, repair or replacement, set out in the notice</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None announced</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">How to claim</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A company contact, website or form in the notice</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No claim process</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">What the CPSC tells owners</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using and contact the company for the remedy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using immediately and dispose of it</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Company&rsquo;s view</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Agreed to the recall</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">&ldquo;The company objects to this press release&rdquo;</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            So when someone says &ldquo;the Sperax recall&rdquo;, the honest correction is that there was no recall, and that
            is precisely the problem: a recall would have come with a way to get your money back or a fixed unit. A warning
            tells you the product is dangerous but leaves you to deal with the cost. That is why the refund steps below go
            through the place you bought it, not through Sperax via the CPSC.
          </p>
          <p>
            The warning does not stop the CPSC from taking further action later, and companies sometimes agree to a recall
            after a warning. If that happens, a recall notice will appear on cpsc.gov with a remedy and a way to claim it.
            As of 25 November 2026, the CPSC listing still shows only the April warning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Sperax Models, and How to Find Your Model Number</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC names four models: <strong>Pro, Q1, RM-01 and RM-02</strong>, sold under the Sperax brand name. It
            describes them as black with red trim, and it says <strong>the model number is located on a label near the power
            switch</strong>.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Unplug the pad first.</strong> Do not switch it on to check anything.</li>
            <li><strong>Find the power switch.</strong> On most walking pads this is a rocker switch near where the cord
            enters the frame, usually at the front end or the side.</li>
            <li><strong>Look for the label next to it.</strong> Read the model line. Write down the model and any serial
            number, and take a clear photo of the label.</li>
            <li><strong>Cross-check your order.</strong> Your order history on Amazon, Walmart, Newegg or eBay will show the
            product title and the seller, which helps if the label is worn.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Label says</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Covered by the CPSC warning?</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to do</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sperax Pro</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; follow the steps below</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sperax Q1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; follow the steps below</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sperax RM-01</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; follow the steps below</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sperax RM-02</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; follow the steps below</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Another Sperax model</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not named in the warning</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check cpsc.gov for updates; watch for the same faults</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Label missing or unreadable</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cannot confirm</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treat it as covered and stop using it</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Many walking pads are sold under more than one listing title, so do not rely on the name of the listing alone;
            the label on the pad is what counts. If you cannot read it, the cautious choice is to treat the pad as one of the
            four, because the downside of being wrong is a fall or a fire.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Owners Should Do, Step by Step</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC&rsquo;s instructions are simple: stop using it, dispose of it, and do not pass it on. The order below adds
            the practical steps that protect your chance of a refund, which you lose if you throw the pad away first.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Stop using it and unplug it today.</strong> Not after one more session. The hazards the CPSC describes
            are a sudden change of speed, an abrupt stop and overheating, and none of these gives warning.</li>
            <li><strong>Store it safely while you sort things out.</strong> Keep it unplugged, away from anything that burns,
            and out of reach of children, who may try to switch it on.</li>
            <li><strong>Gather your evidence.</strong> Photograph the model label, the whole pad and any damage, scorch marks
            or melted plastic. Save your order confirmation and the seller&rsquo;s name.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact the seller or platform</strong> you bought from and ask for a return or refund, citing CPSC
            Product Safety Warning 26-415 of 16 April 2026. More on this below.</li>
            <li><strong>Report any incident</strong> you have had (a sudden speed change, an abrupt stop, a fall, overheating,
            smoke or a burn) at SaferProducts.gov.</li>
            <li><strong>Dispose of it</strong> once any return is settled, in a way that stops anyone else from using it.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If someone in your home has already fallen from the pad or been burned, get medical advice first; the paperwork can
            wait. Then report it, because the CPSC&rsquo;s incident numbers are built from reports like yours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="refund" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Can You Get a Refund?</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC warning announces no remedy, so there is no official refund programme. That does not mean you have no
            options, only that the route goes through whoever sold you the pad. Here is how to approach it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start with the platform&rsquo;s order page.</strong> Amazon, Walmart, Newegg and eBay each have their
            own return and buyer-protection rules, and many Sperax pads were sold by third-party sellers on those sites. We
            cannot confirm any platform&rsquo;s current policy for this warning, so check your own order page and contact
            the platform&rsquo;s customer service rather than relying on a general rule.</li>
            <li><strong>Be specific.</strong> Give the model, the order number and the warning number, and say the CPSC has
            told consumers to stop using and dispose of the product. A short, factual message is more effective than a long
            one.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask what they need before you dispose of it.</strong> Some sellers want the item back, some want a photo
            of it destroyed, and some want nothing. Do not throw it away until you know.</li>
            <li><strong>If the seller says no,</strong> ask the platform itself to review it, and keep a record of every reply.
            If you paid by credit card, your card issuer can tell you whether a dispute is possible; the rules and time limits
            are theirs, not ours.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Be realistic: a pad bought long ago may be outside every return window, and without a recall nobody is obliged to
            refund it. Even then, the CPSC&rsquo;s advice does not change. A walking pad is not worth a concussion or a house
            fire, and a replacement from a brand that stands behind its products costs less than either.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dispose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Dispose of a Sperax Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC says to dispose of the pad and not to sell or give it away. The aim is simple: it should never be used
            again by anyone. A walking pad left whole on the curb is likely to be picked up and used.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Make it unusable first.</strong> Once it is unplugged and any return is settled, cutting off the power
            cord is a simple way to stop anyone plugging it back in. This is our suggestion, not a CPSC instruction.</li>
            <li><strong>Treat it as electronic waste.</strong> A walking pad has a motor, a control board and wiring. Many
            towns and counties have e-waste drop-off days or accept small electrical items at a recycling centre; your local
            waste service will say where it should go.</li>
            <li><strong>Take out any remote batteries</strong> and recycle them separately.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not list it for parts.</strong> &ldquo;For parts or repair&rdquo; listings are still a sale, and the
            CPSC says not to sell these products.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to dispose of a treadmill</a> guide covers bulky-item pickup, recycling centres and haul-away options in more detail; a walking pad is smaller
            and lighter, so most of it is easier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="report" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Report a Problem to the CPSC</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC&rsquo;s warning asks people to report incidents involving Sperax walking pads or treadmills, or any
            product-related injury, at <strong>SaferProducts.gov</strong>. That site is run by the CPSC and describes itself
            as the place &ldquo;where the public can file and read complaints&rdquo; about consumer products. You can also
            search it for existing reports before you buy anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            A useful report includes:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The brand and model exactly as the label shows it (for example, Sperax RM-01)</li>
            <li>Where and when you bought it</li>
            <li>What happened: a sudden speed change, an abrupt stop, overheating, smoke, fire, a fall or a burn</li>
            <li>Any injury and whether it needed medical attention</li>
            <li>Photos of the label and of any damage</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you prefer to phone, SaferProducts.gov lists the CPSC&rsquo;s toll-free number, 800-638-2772, and a TTY line,
            301-595-7054. Reporting is worth doing even if you were not hurt: the CPSC&rsquo;s figures of 201 speed and
            stopping reports and 573 heat reports exist because owners took the time to file them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying or Selling a Used Sperax</h2>
<p>
            The CPSC says not to sell or give away these products, so the second-hand market is where a warned pad is most
            likely to hurt someone new. A buyer on a marketplace has no way of knowing the history.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>If you are buying used,</strong> skip any Sperax Pro, Q1, RM-01 or RM-02, at any price. Ask the seller
            for a photo of the model label before you travel to see any walking pad.</li>
            <li><strong>If you are selling,</strong> do not list one of these models. If you see one listed, you can let the
            seller know about the CPSC warning.</li>
            <li><strong>If you were given one,</strong> the same advice applies: stop using it and dispose of it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/where-to-buy-a-cheap-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a cheap treadmill</a> guide has a short checklist for checking any used machine against CPSC recalls and warnings before you pay.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hazards" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Signs of the Same Hazards on Any Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            The faults in the Sperax warning, sudden speed changes, abrupt stops and overheating, are not unique to one
            brand, and on any pad they are a reason to stop, not to push through. These are the signs to take seriously,
            whatever you own.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Sign</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Why it matters</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to do</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed changes on its own</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">You can lose your footing without warning</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; contact the brand</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt stops abruptly mid-step</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Your body keeps moving when the belt does not</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using; rule out an auto-stop timer in the manual first</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Burning or hot-plastic smell</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">An overheating motor, board or cord</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Switch off, unplug, let it cool; do not restart until checked</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hot cord or plug</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A wiring or connection fault</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unplug; stop using any extension lead</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Smoke, sparks or scorch marks</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">An active fire risk</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unplug if safe; do not use again</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Some of these have harmless causes on a sound pad: an auto-stop timer, a dry belt that makes a pad hesitate, or a
            safety magnet that has slipped. Our <a href="/walking-pad-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad not working</a> guide sorts
            the fixable faults from the ones that need the brand, and <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill burning smell</a> and <a href="/treadmill-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill overheating</a> cover heat in
            depth. If the safety key or magnet on another pad does not stop the belt as it should, see <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety key not working</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safe-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Walking Pad Safety Habits That Help on Any Brand</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Plug it straight into a wall outlet</strong> where the manual says so, not into a light-duty extension
            lead or a crowded power strip. See <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill extension cords</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the safety key or magnet</strong> every time, clipped to your clothing where the pad has one, so
            the belt stops if you drift off the back.</li>
            <li><strong>Unplug it when you are done.</strong> A pad left on standby under a desk all day is out of sight and
            out of mind.</li>
            <li><strong>Give the motor air.</strong> Thick carpet and clutter around the motor housing trap heat; a firm mat
            on a hard floor is better.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep children and pets away</strong> while it runs and when it is stored, and keep the key or remote
            where small hands cannot reach it.</li>
            <li><strong>Stay within the stated weight limit</strong> and use it at walking pace unless the maker says it is
            built for running.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a> page has the
            full list for home machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Choosing a Safer Replacement</h2>
<p>
            No brand can promise a pad will never fail, but you can stack the odds. Before you buy a replacement, check:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The brand and model on cpsc.gov.</strong> Search the CPSC&rsquo;s recalls and product safety warnings
            for the brand name before you order. It takes a minute.</li>
            <li><strong>A named brand with US support.</strong> A working support contact and a stated warranty matter when
            something goes wrong.</li>
            <li><strong>A safety-testing mark.</strong> Look for a listing mark from a recognised testing laboratory on the
            product or in the listing, such as UL, ETL or CSA, and check the listing actually claims it for that model.</li>
            <li><strong>A safety key or magnet,</strong> and a clear stop button on the pad or remote.</li>
            <li><strong>A handrail, if balance is a concern.</strong> Pads with a fold-up handrail give you something to hold
            if the belt does change speed. See <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with handrail</a>,
            and for older walkers, <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads for seniors</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> guide and the <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a> go through
            capacity, speed, warranty and support for current models from other brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stay-updated" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Check for Updates</h2>
<p>
            Warnings can be followed by recalls, and new warnings appear every month. The reliable place to check is the
            source itself: the &ldquo;Recalls &amp; Product Safety Warnings&rdquo; section of cpsc.gov, where you can search
            by brand. This page reflects the CPSC listing as of 25 November 2026. If Sperax or its importer agrees to a
            recall, the notice on cpsc.gov will set out the remedy and how to claim it, and that notice, not this page or a
            seller&rsquo;s message, is what to follow.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be wary of emails or messages claiming to offer a &ldquo;Sperax recall refund&rdquo; that ask for payment or card
            details. A genuine CPSC recall notice is published on cpsc.gov, so check there before acting on any message.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes After the Sperax Warning</h2>
          <p>Each of these either keeps the risk in your home or costs you a refund.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Waiting for a recall before stopping</h3>
              <p>There may never be one. The CPSC says to stop using these models now; unplug it today and deal with the refund separately.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Throwing it out before asking the seller</h3>
              <p>Some sellers want the item back or a photo of it first. Contact them with your order number and the warning number before you dispose of it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Selling it or giving it away</h3>
              <p>The CPSC says not to. Passing it on moves the fall and fire risk to someone who does not know about it. Make it unusable and recycle it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the listing name over the label</h3>
              <p>Listing titles vary. Check the model label near the power switch; if it is unreadable, treat the pad as covered.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The fix, in short</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">There is no Sperax recall: the CPSC issued a warning on 16 April 2026 telling owners of the
            Pro, Q1, RM-01 and RM-02 to stop using them immediately and dispose of them, because the importer refused to agree
            to an acceptable recall.</strong> That means no official refund, repair or replacement, so the route to your money
            is the seller or platform you bought from.
          </p>
          <p>
            Unplug it today, photograph the label, contact the seller before you throw anything away, report any incident at
            SaferProducts.gov, and do not sell or give it away. When you replace it, check the new brand on cpsc.gov first; our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best walking pads</a> guide is a good
            place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
