        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is there a Sperax walking pad recall?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. On 16 April 2026 the CPSC issued a product safety warning, not a recall, telling consumers to stop using the Sperax Pro, Q1, RM-01 and RM-02 immediately and dispose of them. The CPSC says the importer refused to agree to an acceptable recall.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which Sperax models are affected?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The CPSC names four: Pro, Q1, RM-01 and RM-02. The model number is on a label near the power switch. The warning does not mention other Sperax models.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why did the CPSC warn about Sperax walking pads?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The CPSC says they pose fall, burn and fire hazards. It cites 201 reports of uncontrollable speed, abrupt stops and similar failures, with at least 66 falls or injuries including at least one concussion, and 573 reports of overheating, fires and other thermal incidents, including four reports of minor burns.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I get a refund for my Sperax walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no official remedy because there is no recall. Contact the seller or platform you bought from with your order number and the CPSC warning number (26-415), and ask what they need before you dispose of the pad. Any refund is at their discretion.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I keep using my Sperax walking pad carefully?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The CPSC says to stop using it immediately. The faults it describes, sudden speed changes, abrupt stops and overheating, happen without warning, so careful use does not remove the risk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I sell my Sperax walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. The CPSC says not to sell or give away these products. Make it unusable and dispose of it as electronic waste instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where do I report a problem with a Sperax walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At SaferProducts.gov, the CPSC's public reporting site, which the warning names. You can also phone the CPSC on 800-638-2772.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

